{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#519","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#519","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#519\",\"parent_revision\":\"a9e827e21fd070118bd9b39b50486724bba34c01f35d8552506d28805ddb1500\",\"root_revision\":\"a9e827e21fd070118bd9b39b50486724bba34c01f35d8552506d28805ddb1500\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe repo has shared GitHub API interaction scattered across multiple scripts and plugins (backlog.py, daily-releases scripts, etc.), each with its own inline GitHub client construction, SSL handling, and token management. Success: a single shared github_utils library installable by any script or plugin in the repo via PEP 723 [tool.uv.sources] or a proper package dependency, so all GitHub API work goes through one place. Done when all existing inline GitHub client code is replaced with imports from the shared library and CI stays green.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Claim:** The dh backlog MCP server and SAM MCP server cannot reach GitHub in an agent session.\\n  **verdict:** VERIFIED\\n  **Evidence:** `backlog_list` and `sam_plan` both return\\n  `SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] ... CA cert does not\\n  include key usage extension'))`.\\n\\n- **Claim:** The cause is `VERIFY_X509_STRICT`, not the CA bundle path and not the token.\\n  **verdict:** VERIFIED\\n  **Evidence:** A controlled probe varying only that flag, against the same CA file, host and\\n  proxy, produced `strict=True -> FAIL: CA cert does not include key usage extension` and\\n  `strict=False-> OK`. `SSL_CERT_FILE` and `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` were already set correctly.\\n\\n- **Claim:** Authentication is injected by the proxy, so no token handling change is needed.\\n  **verdict:** VERIFIED\\n  **Evidence:** A request with no `Authorization` header returns HTTP 200 with\\n  `x-ratelimit-limit: 15000`; anonymous GitHub is 60. `gh auth status` reports\\n  `The token in GITHUB_TOKEN is invalid` while `gh api` calls succeed. PyGithub succeeds with a\\n  deliberately fake 40-character token.\\n\\n- **Claim:** The existing `make_github_client()` in daily-releases cannot fix this.\\n  **verdict:** VERIFIED\\n  **Evidence:** It passes `verify=<path>`, and\\n  `Github(auth=..., verify=\\\"/root/.ccr/ca-bundle.crt\\\")` still fails with `SSLError`. A CA path\\n  does not clear the strict flag.\\n\\n- **Claim:** PyGithub exposes a public hook that can carry a custom TLS context.\\n  **verdict:** VERIFIED\\n  **Evidence:** PyGithub 2.9.0's `HTTPSRequestsConnectionClass.__init__` builds a\\n  `requests.Session` and mounts a `requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter`;\\n  `Requester.injectConnectionClasses` substitutes the class process-wide. A patched class made\\n  `get_repo` and `get_issue` succeed through the proxy.\\n\\n- **Claim:** Overriding `init_poolmanager` alone is sufficient.\\n  **verdict:** REFUTED\\n  **Evidence:** `requests` builds a separate ProxyManager via `proxy_manager_for`, which\\n  ignores that context. A probe overriding only `init_poolmanager` failed on all four cases;\\n  adding `proxy_manager_for` turned all of them into HTTP 200.\\n\\n- **Claim:** `injectConnectionClasses` disabling connection reuse is a material cost.\\n  **verdict:** REFUTED\\n  **Evidence:** Measured over 12 sequential calls through the proxy: 660 ms/call without reuse\\n  against 637 ms/call with it, a 3% difference that PyGithub's own 250 ms inter-request delay\\n  dominates. Restoring reuse would require writing a name-mangled private attribute, so the\\n  reuse stays off and the module stays on public API only.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Date:** 2026-09-13\\n\\n**Decision:** APPROVED\\n\\n| # | Condition | Status | Source |\\n|---|-----------|--------|--------|\\n| 1 | Identified scripts with inline GitHub clients | AVAILABLE | Repository-wide grep: 11 call sites in 10 files |\\n| 2 | Shared library location strategy | AVAILABLE | Resolved above \u2014 canonical module in `backlog_core/`, path-loaded by repo scripts |\\n| 3 | `backlog_core` as reference | AVAILABLE | `gh_client.py` is the established client |\\n| 4 | Why the consolidation is load-bearing | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check above: every inline client currently fails behind the proxy |\\n| 5 | The mechanism a shared factory must encapsulate | AVAILABLE | Verified end to end against PyGithub 2.9.0 |\\n\\n**Note:** condition 2 was MISSING in the 2026-04-23 assessment. It is now resolved by the\\ndistribution-boundary analysis above.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type:** missing-guardrail\\n\\n**Rationale:** The item was filed as a Refactor about duplication. Investigation found the\\nduplication is currently masking a live outage: every inline client shares the same missing\\nTLS configuration, and all of them fail in any agent session behind the TLS-intercepting\\negress proxy. A single shared factory is the guardrail that makes the configuration\\nimpossible to omit.\\n\\n**Analysis method:** Direct source read of all 11 call sites, plus controlled TLS probes at\\nthree layers (raw `ssl`, `requests`, PyGithub).\\n\\n**Scenario target:** A contributor adds a new GitHub-calling script and it silently works\\nlocally while failing in every agent session.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Systems Inventory**\\n\\n| System | Path | Change |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Shared factory (new) | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_client.py` | Token lookup, CA bundle detection, TLS install, client construction |\\n| Backlog client | `backlog_core/gh_client.py` (2 sites) | Replace inline `Github(...)` with the factory |\\n| Artifact provider | `backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` | Same |\\n| SAM context backend | `sam_schema/core/backends/github_context_backend.py` | Same |\\n| daily-releases | `.claude/skills/daily-releases/scripts/daily_releases_lib/.../github_utils.py` | Replace `get_ssl_verify()`/`make_github_client()`; drop the `GITHUB_SSL_VERIFY=false` branch |\\n| gh skill scripts | `.claude/skills/gh/scripts/github_project_setup.py`, `experiment_cleanup.py` | Same |\\n| repo scripts | `.claude/scripts/repair_from_original_register.py`, `sync_issues_to_project.py`, `rebuild_issue_bodies.py` | Same |\\n| knowledge explorer | `research/knowledge-explorer.py` | Same |\\n\\n**Distribution boundary \u2014 resolves RT-ICA condition 2**\\n\\nTwo distribution boundaries exist, and one file cannot sit in both:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/` ships to the plugin cache and must work when installed from\\n  the marketplace, where the repo root does not exist. `run_backlog_server.py` puts the plugin\\n  root on `sys.path`, so `backlog_core.*` is importable there.\\n- `.claude/**` and `research/*.py` are PEP 723 scripts that run from the repo checkout and\\n  cannot import `backlog_core`.\\n\\n**Decision:** the canonical module lives at `backlog_core/github_client.py` with zero\\n`backlog_core`-internal imports, so it ships with the plugin and stays loadable by file path.\\nRepo-side PEP 723 scripts reach the same file through the established\\n`importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` pattern already used by `_load_backlink_lib()`, which\\navoids importing `backlog_core/__init__.py`. One implementation, no copy.\\n\\n**Risk:** Medium. The module changes TLS verification behaviour for every GitHub caller in the\\nrepo. The relaxation is narrow \u2014 chain and hostname verification stay on, only\\n`VERIFY_X509_STRICT` is cleared \u2014 and it applies only when a CA bundle variable names a real\\nfile on disk. On an ordinary network the strict default is untouched.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Priority:** P1\\n\\n**Effort:** M \u2014 one new module, 11 call sites, one new test module, no new dependency.\\n\\n**Priority rationale:** filed as P2 when it read as duplication cleanup. The verified finding is\\nthat the whole dh backlog and SAM toolchain is unusable in any agent session behind the proxy,\\nwhich is a P1 condition.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Alignment assessment:** ALIGNED\\n\\n`AGENTS.md` requires fixing root causes over patching symptoms and avoiding silent failure paths\\n(`rules/silent-failure-prevention.md`). A single factory removes the class of defect rather than\\none instance. Removing the `GITHUB_SSL_VERIFY=false` branch also removes a way to disable\\nverification silently, which `rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` argues against.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. A shared module exposes client construction, token lookup, and CA bundle detection in one place.\\n2. The module keeps certificate-chain and hostname verification on, and clears only\\n   `VERIFY_X509_STRICT`.\\n3. The relaxation applies only when a CA bundle environment variable names an existing file.\\n4. The module offers no option that disables verification outright.\\n5. The module uses PyGithub public API only \u2014 no name-mangled private attributes.\\n6. The install is idempotent and safe from more than one thread.\\n7. All four `plugins/development-harness/` call sites route through the module.\\n8. At least one repo-side PEP 723 script routes through the same module by path, proving the\\n   single-implementation claim.\\n9. `get_ssl_verify()`'s `GITHUB_SSL_VERIFY=false` branch is removed, because nothing needs it once\\n   the module exists.\\n10. Tests cover: strict-flag clearing, CA-absent no-op, idempotency, and token resolution order.\\n11. `uv run pytest`, `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, and `ty check` all pass.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Run any GitHub-calling script in an agent session behind the TLS-intercepting proxy.\\n2. Observe `SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate\\n   verify failed: CA cert does not include key usage extension'))`.\\n3. Confirm the flag is the cause: build an `ssl` context against the same CA bundle, clear only\\n   `ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT`, and observe the same connection succeed.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **consolidate repo-wide github api utilities into a shared library** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. **This change relaxes a TLS verification setting in shared infrastructure.** It needs explicit\\n   human approval before it merges, independently of the narrowness of the relaxation.\\n2. The TLS fix restores REST only. GitHub GraphQL and non-repository-scoped REST paths such as\\n   `search/issues` stay blocked by proxy policy, with `HTTP 403` and an explicit message. Any code\\n   path reaching `_GraphQLRequester` in `gh_client.py` still fails and needs a REST fallback.\\n\\n\\n---\\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPython 3.13 enables `ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT` by default. The agent proxy's CA carries no\\n`keyUsage` extension, so strict verification rejects it with\\n`CA cert does not include key usage extension`. The failure lands in the TLS handshake,\\nbefore any request byte leaves the socket, so it is unrelated to tokens or headers.\\n\\n`gh` reaches the same API through the same proxy without trouble because it is Go, whose\\nverifier does not impose that extension check.\\n\\nEach call site constructs `Github(auth=Auth.Token(token))` inline with no TLS configuration,\\nso there is no single place where the fix can be applied once.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-09-13)\\n\",\"digest\":\"4689fc03973f67fa53cf778729b93deffa90c57143b8bc7d2dd8f18bf8ba9b13\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABUeTNEA\"}"}